# Git Flow – mandatory for this repository

This project uses the classic **Git Flow model** (Vincent Driessen).
Every coherent piece of work is done on its own branch – never directly on
`main` or `develop`.

## Long-lived branches

| Branch    | Purpose                                                                  |
|-----------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `main`    | Released versions only. Every release commit has a tag `vX.Y.Z`.         |
| `develop` | Integration branch for the next version. Always green (`npm run check`). |

Nothing is ever committed directly to `main` or `develop` – they are only merged into.

## Clean history: rebase + squash

`main` and `develop` have a **linear history without merge commits** (enforced on GitHub
by a ruleset; only squash merges are allowed).

- Before merging, a feature/bugfix branch is **rebased** onto the current `develop`
  (`git rebase develop`); `develop` is never merged into the branch.
- It lands on `develop` as **exactly one squash commit**. Subject in Conventional Commits
  format with the branch number at the end: `feat(render): add texture mapping (GEO-30)`.
  The body summarises the change and names the new tests.
- Releases and hotfixes reach `main` by **fast-forward** (`--ff-only`) (see Workflow).
- External contributions arrive only as pull requests from a **fork** against `develop`
  (see `CONTRIBUTING.md`) and are merged on GitHub with "Squash and merge".

## Supporting branches

| Type     | Branches off | Merges into                     | Name                            | Used for |
|----------|--------------|---------------------------------|---------------------------------|----------|
| Feature  | `develop`    | `develop`                       | `feature/GEO-<nr>-<short-name>` | New functionality, refactoring, tooling, docs |
| Bugfix   | `develop`    | `develop`                       | `bugfix/GEO-<nr>-<short-name>`  | A bug that is **not** released yet |
| Release  | `develop`    | `main` **and** `develop` (ff)   | `release/<X.Y.Z>`               | Prepare a version: version number, CHANGELOG, last fixes |
| Hotfix   | `main`       | `main` (ff) **and** `develop`\* | `hotfix/<X.Y.Z>`                | Urgent fix of a released version |
| Support  | `main`       | –                               | `support/<X.Y>`                 | Only if an old major version has to be maintained |

\* Into `develop` by cherry-pick. If a `release/*` branch is open, the hotfix goes there instead.

### Naming rules

- `<nr>` is sequential and unique (GEO-1, GEO-2, …). A number is taken as soon as it appears
  in **any** branch (including remote and deleted ones) or commit subject. Highest number:
  ```bash
  { git ls-remote --heads origin; git branch -a; git log --all --format=%s; } \
    | grep -oiE 'GEO[-_][0-9]+' | grep -oE '[0-9]+' | sort -n | tail -1
  ```
  Known legacy: GEO-08 is used twice (`feature/GEO-08_Fixing_TS_Stuff` from 2022 and
  `feature/GEO-08-workflow-rules`).
- `<short-name>` in kebab-case, English, short: `feature/GEO-12-math-core`.
- Versions follow **SemVer**. Before 1.0.0, minor versions may contain breaking changes.

## Legacy branches

Frozen branches from before v0.1.0. They are kept for archival purposes, contain a
`LEGACY.md` and are **never** merged, rebased or developed further – they are built on
the old API that was replaced in v0.1.0.

| Branch                             | Date       | Content                                       |
|------------------------------------|------------|-----------------------------------------------|
| `feature/GEO-08_Fixing_TS_Stuff`   | 2023-01-14 | TS fixes/refactoring of the old API           |
| `feature/subdivide`                | 2023-05-31 | Subdivision for `Geo3D` (old API)             |

Ideas from them are – if wanted – re-implemented in a new `feature/GEO-<nr>-…` from
`develop`. New legacy branches: commit a `LEGACY.md` and list them here.

## Workflow

```bash
# Start a feature
git checkout develop && git pull
git checkout -b feature/GEO-12-math-core

# … commit …

# Finish a feature
npm run check                                    # typecheck, lint, format, tests, build
git push -u origin feature/GEO-12-math-core      # ALWAYS push before merging → CI runs on the branch
# … wait for the branch's CI run; if red, fix and push again, do not merge …
git checkout develop && git pull
git checkout feature/GEO-12-math-core && git rebase develop   # if develop moved: rebase, push again
git push --force-with-lease                                    # (own feature branch only!) and wait for CI
git checkout develop
git merge --squash feature/GEO-12-math-core
git commit -m "feat(math): add math core (GEO-12)" -m "…summary, new tests…"
git push origin develop
git branch -D feature/GEO-12-math-core              # -D: after a squash the branch is not "merged"
git push origin --delete feature/GEO-12-math-core   # clean up the remote branch after the merge

# Release
git checkout -b release/0.2.0 develop
npm version 0.2.0 --no-git-tag-version   # CHANGELOG: [Unreleased] → [0.2.0] - YYYY-MM-DD
git commit -am "chore(release): 0.2.0"
git checkout main    && git merge --ff-only release/0.2.0 && git tag -a v0.2.0 -m "v0.2.0"
git checkout develop && git merge --ff-only release/0.2.0
git branch -d release/0.2.0
# If --ff-only on main is not possible (main got a hotfix since the last release):
#   git merge --squash release/0.2.0 && git commit -m 'chore(release): 0.2.0'

# Hotfix
git checkout -b hotfix/0.2.1 main
# … fix + version number + CHANGELOG …
# The hotfix branch has exactly one commit (squash it first if needed)
git checkout main    && git merge --ff-only hotfix/0.2.1 && git tag -a v0.2.1 -m "v0.2.1"
git checkout develop && git cherry-pick hotfix/0.2.1
```

With the `git-flow` CLI (AVH) installed, for starting branches and naming: `git flow init -d`,
then `git flow feature start GEO-12-math-core` (finish with a squash as above, not
`feature finish`), `git flow release start 0.2.0`, `git flow hotfix start 0.2.1`
(prefixes: `feature/`, `bugfix/`, `release/`, `hotfix/`, `support/`, tag prefix `v`).

## Commits

- **Conventional Commits**, subject in English, imperative mood, at most ~72 characters:
  `feat(math): add Mat4.lookAt`, `fix(svg): disable default fill`,
  `refactor:`, `test:`, `docs:`, `build:`, `ci:`, `chore:`.
- Breaking changes: `feat!:` or a footer `BREAKING CHANGE: …`.
- One commit = one logical change. Typecheck and tests should be green after every commit.

### Author and commit footer (mandatory, also for AI agents)

- Author **and** committer of every commit: **`Jan <log@janwalenda.de>`** – no other identity,
  not even the agent's. Check or set it before the first commit of a session:
  `git config user.name "Jan" && git config user.email "log@janwalenda.de"`.
- **No** `Co-Authored-By:` lines in commit messages (not for Claude/Cursor either).
- Applies to squash commits and tags as well (tagger = Jan).

## Rules for AI agents (Claude Code, Cursor)

- Before any change, check which branch you are on. For new work **always** create a
  matching `feature/` or `bugfix/` branch from `develop`.
  Related code belongs in **one** branch; unrelated changes in separate ones.
- **No feature and no fix without tests** – see `testing.mdc`. A `feature/` or `bugfix/` branch
  that changes code is not merged into `develop` without matching new/updated tests.
- Before merging into `develop`: `npm run check` must be green.
- **Every `feature/`, `bugfix/` and `hotfix/` branch is pushed before it is merged**
  (`git push -u origin <branch>`), so it is visible on GitHub and CI runs on it.
  Merge only once the branch's CI run is green. Also applies to docs/rules-only branches.
- After the merge, delete the branch locally **and** on the remote. If the environment does not
  allow deleting the remote branch, tell the user instead of working around it.
- **Never** push to `main`, finish releases, push tags or run `npm publish`
  without the user's explicit approval.
- **Never** force-push `develop` or `main` or rewrite their history. Force-push
  (`--force-with-lease`) only to your own feature/bugfix branch after a rebase.
- Never run external pull requests (from forks) locally before reading the diff –
  especially changes to `.github/`, `package.json`, the lockfile, build and test configuration.
